This research introduces SQUID, an open-source tool for developing and training hybrid Quantum-Classical classification algorithms. It utilizes PyTorch for the classical components, allowing for the creation of multiple quantum models that can be optimized with back-propagation. We demonstrate SQUID's capabilities by showcasing its implementation in a standard binary classification task from the widely-used MNIST dataset. Notably, we discuss how the choice of output affects the scalability of quantum models when utilizing gradient-based optimization. Experimental results highlight SQUID's capabilities, especially its efficiency in training and adapting to changes in the input dataset. Overall, this paper explores the effectiveness of a particular Quantum-Classical framework, while also discussing the challenges and considerations that must be addressed for the practical application of such approaches. Additionally, we discuss the future research directions that could lead to more reliable and robust outcomes. Finally, to facilitate further innovation, we release an extension of the SQUID library to support teaching hybrid Quantum-Classical systems. You can find more information at https://sqid-ml.org. 

